/* The header for this test is at the BOTTOM of this file, below the script.
   It is there because a constant inside a leading comment can swallow the
   rest of the file in the engine - see RMSTEST_56 and the directory README.
   Nothing below the script can do any harm. */

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain DESERT

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>

  /* ARM 1 - the mixing model. Six tree types share this terrain and the
     engine's rule for choosing between them is unmeasured. */
  create_terrain SOUTH_AMERICAN_FOREST
  {
    base_terrain DESERT
    land_percent 40
    number_of_clumps 12
  }

  /* ARM 2 - the density unit. Free second observable: this terrain is the
     one whose density is NOT 1000, so it is where per-mille shows itself. */
  create_terrain DLC_BAOBABFOREST
  {
    base_terrain DESERT
    land_percent 25
    number_of_clumps 10
  }

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

/*
================================================================================
RMSTEST_65 - the forest auto-spawn mixing model, and the density unit
================================================================================

DESERT is the base because it is one of the few terrains with NO terrain_unit
slots of its own, so every object in the export came from one of the two arms.
GRASS and DIRT would each have added a cosmetic unit to the histogram.

The two arms share NO unit, checked against the dat, so they can sit on one map
and be read independently from a single map-wide object histogram.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ARM 1 - WHICH TREE, WHEN A TERRAIN LISTS SIX
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The dat gives terrain 128 six terrain_unit slots, in this order, with these
densities: ITALIAN_PINETREE 350, OLIVE_TREE 150, TREE_MONKEY_PUZZLE 300,
DLC_ACACIATREE 420, TREE_GREEN_OAK 750, DLC_AFRICANBUSH 1000. It does not say
how the engine picks. Four models are plausible and they disagree loudly.

Read each count as a PERCENTAGE OF THE TERRAIN-128 TILE COUNT, which the probe
prints in its own terrain histogram. Absolute counts depend on how much land
the two commands actually won, which this test does not care about.

  unit                    A seq   B last-wins   C normalised   D truncated
  ITALIAN_PINETREE        35.0%       0%           11.8%          35.0%
  OLIVE_TREE               9.8%       0%            5.1%          15.0%
  TREE_MONKEY_PUZZLE      16.6%       0%           10.1%          30.0%
  DLC_ACACIATREE          16.2%       0%           14.1%          20.0%
  TREE_GREEN_OAK          16.8%       0%           25.3%           0%
  DLC_AFRICANBUSH          5.6%     100%           33.7%           0%
  E[wood/tile]            108.1     100.0          107.1          110.0

  A - sequential first-hit down the slot list. p_i = remaining * d_i/1000.
      This is what the plan currently assumes.
  B - independent rolls, a later slot overwriting an earlier one.
  C - densities as normalised weights.
  D - each slot claims d_i/1000 of the tiles in order, truncated at 100%.

TREE_GREEN_OAK and DLC_AFRICANBUSH are the discriminators - every model puts
them somewhere different, and two models put one of them at exactly zero. Read
those two first; the other four only confirm.

C AND B ARE ALREADY REFUTED ON PAPER and are here as controls, so a reading
that lands on either means the test itself is wrong, not the model. C would
give DLC_BAOBABFOREST 100% tree coverage when its own descriptiveName says
25%. B cannot produce a mixture at all, yet MEDITERRANEAN_FOREST's
descriptiveName calls it "mixture of cypress, olive and italian pine".

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ARM 2 - IS terrain_unit_density PER-MILLE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Terrain 49 has ONE slot, DLC_BAOBABTREE at density 250, and its own
descriptiveName in game-constants.json says "200 wood per tree; 25% tree
density". Reading 1000 as 100% is currently supported only by that prose
agreeing with two other rows. This is the first direct look.

  count(DLC_BAOBABTREE) / count(terrain 49 tiles)

  0.25  confirms per-mille. Nothing changes; the plan's numbers stand.
  1.00  the density field is not a coverage fraction at all, and every
        forest-wood figure in the plan needs rederiving before it ships.
  other refutes per-mille and gives the real scale directly.

EXPECT THIS TO READ SLIGHTLY LOW. The engine clears trees around player
starts, so some Baobab tiles lose theirs. ARM 1 is immune - clearing removes
species in proportion, so the ratios it measures do not move. If arm 2 comes
back near 0.22-0.25 treat it as confirmed; if it comes back near 1.0 or near
0.10 that is a real result, not clearing.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), TWO PLAYERS. ONE RUN.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Two players rather than eight to keep start-clearing off arm 2. One run is
enough: even a few hundred tiles separates the four models by many standard
errors, since the tightest real gap (A's 9.8% against D's 15.0% on OLIVE_TREE)
is five percentage points.

If the terrain histogram shows either terrain below ~200 tiles, raise
land_percent and regenerate rather than reading it.

If DLC_AFRICANBUSH reads 0 while TREE_GREEN_OAK reads sensibly, suspect the
constant before believing model D - that unit is shared with terrains 20 and
89 and is the least independently proven name in the list.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>"
  The two default histograms are the whole reading: objects give arm 1's
  numerator and arm 2's numerator, terrains give both denominators.
*/
